\subsection{The knowledge items}
\label{app:knowledge-items}

The knowledge items are written for this study and published here in
full.
Each item tests one candidate critical aspect from the chapters: whether
the student discerns it is exactly what the item measures.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do not
point out the relevant aspect---discerning it is what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}---so each stem shows a concrete command and its
behaviour and leaves the interpretation to the student.
Each distractor expresses a documented misconception or a plausible
consequence of leaving the aspect undiscerned; the prose before each
item names the aspect it tests and why the distractors are the mistakes
they are.
All items weigh one point: we measure knowledge by counting correct
answers.

The items are numbered from position 11, so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps
are fine, and the numbering leaves room for the quiz-specific opening
item (consent or preparation) before them.

<<knowledge items>>=
<<item: the command line reads a language>>,
<<item: a message after a command>>,
<<item: the same files from two sides>>,
<<item: which directory the command uses>>,
<<item: two names for one file>>,
<<item: sending output into a pipe>>,
<<item: the same sort after different programs>>,
<<item: which computer answers>>,
<<item: switching between machines>>,
<<item: where the remote file ends up>>
@

\subsubsection{The command model}

The command line is a language, not a search box (\cref{sec:shell}): the
shell parses the line into a command and its arguments; it does not guess
what the words mean.
The item gives a line of ordinary English and asks what happens.
The correct answer reads the first word as the name of a program to run;
the search-box distractor treats the line as a query over the machine;
the remaining distractors credit the shell with understanding the words
or silently correcting them.

<<item: the command line reads a language>>=
