\subsubsection{The file system}

Every shell has a current working directory, and a command acts relative
to it (\cref{sec:filesystem}).
The item puts the file in one directory and the shell in another, and
lets the student explain the resulting error.
The correct answer connects the failure to where the command looked; the
distractors deny the file exists at all, misread what [[ls]] can take, or
invoke the graphical editor---each a way of not discerning that the
command acted from the working directory.

